FINAL ANSWER:
Bus factor is **1** at the 50% threshold and **2** at the 80% threshold. I counted lines that still exist in `src/` (8 files, 4,205 lines) using `git blame -w -M -C`. That ignores whitespace-only changes and follows lines moved or copied between files.

| Author | Lines | Share | Cumulative |
|---|---|---|---|
| Andrew Gallant | 2,554 | 60.7% | 60.7% |
| Niko Alvarez | 883 | 21.0% | 81.7% |
| Ola Berg (two identities merged) | 113 | 2.7% | 84.4% |
| Sebastian Thiel | 74 | 1.8% | 86.2% |
| Ashley / Ashley Mannix (merged) | 72 | 1.7% | 87.9% |
| Aron Griffis | 59 | 1.4% | 89.3% |
| Remaining ~30 authors | 450 | 10.7% | 100% |

- **Concentration:** Gallant alone wrote a majority of the surviving lines. Gallant and Alvarez together wrote 81.7%. No one else holds more than 2.7%.
- **Long tail:** About 35 other people each own a small number of lines. Most own fewer than 50, so no one else could realistically take over a file.
- **Identity merging:** I merged names that looked like the same person: `Ola Berg` and `ola.berg`, `Niv Kaminer` and `nivkner`, `Ashley` and `Ashley Mannix`, and `Alisha` and `Alisha Aneja`. This was a guess from the names, and there is no `.mailmap` to confirm it. Merging changes the long tail but not the top two.
- **Thresholds:** The 50% and 80% cutoffs are conventional. The result is the same at 50% and 80% even if some of the merges are wrong.

I only ran the aggregate across `src/`. I can break it down per file to show which files depend on one person. Say so if you want that.

