 - Bash: git log -1 --format=%cd && git log --reverse --format='%h %ad %ae' --date=short | head -3 && git shortlog -sne HEAD | head
FINAL ANSWER:
**Bus factor is 1 by surviving lines.** One author wrote about 61% of the 4,205 lines now in `src/`, and two authors together wrote about 82%.

I ran `git blame -w -M -C` on the 8 tracked files in `src/` and counted the author of each current line:

| Author | Lines | Share |
|---|---|---|
| <email redacted> (Andrew Gallant) | 2,554 | 60.7% |
| <email redacted> | 883 | 21.0% |
| All other 32 authors | 768 | 18.3% |

- **Top author:** Andrew Gallant alone passes 50% (bus factor 1).
- **Top two:** the two together reach 81.7% (bus factor 2 at an 80% threshold).
- **Everyone else:** no one else holds more than 74 lines (1.8%). The next largest are <email redacted> (74), <email redacted> (68) and <email redacted> (59).

**Caveats**
- I merged `<email redacted>` (58 lines) and `<email redacted>` (55 lines) into one person with 113 lines (2.7%). Other emails that belong to one person would also need merging. I didn't check for them.
- I didn't check who is still active. The last commit is 2026-04-09. If Andrew Gallant is no longer working on this code, `src/` has no one who knows most of it. If <email redacted> is also gone, about 82% of it has no active owner.
- Lines count equally, including comments and blanks. `-w`, `-M` and `-C` make blame ignore whitespace changes and follow moved or copied lines. Even so, a bulk reformat or file move can still credit lines to the wrong person.
- This is the whole of `src/`, not a per-file breakdown. If you want to know which of the 8 files are single-owner, I can run it per file.

I didn't use the `busfactor` skill. It weights commits with a decay over time, so it doesn't measure surviving lines.

